62	<div class="wrap">
63	  <span class="eyebrow">Nxeon Shared Hosting</span>
64	  <h1>The <span class="g">website-first</span> re-foundation</h1>
65	  <p class="sub">A complete restructure — logic, data model, and experience — to make managing one site (or ten) easier than any competitor. Simple by default, power on tap.</p>
66	
67	  <!-- 1 -->
68	  <section>
69	    <h2><span class="n">1</span>The core reframe</h2>
70	    <p class="lead">Today one <span class="mono">HostingAccount</span> is secretly three things at once — the customer, the plan, and a "primary" website. That fusion is why everything orbits a primary domain and feels cluttered. We split them.</p>
71	    <div class="ba">
72	      <div class="card"><span class="tag old">Today</span>
73	        <h4>Account = primary domain</h4>
74	        <p>Subdomains nest under it (<span class="mono">shop.you.sites…</span>), email pins to it, every screen tries to be account + plan + site at once.</p>
75	      </div>
76	      <div class="arrow">→</div>
77	      <div class="card"><span class="tag new">Restructured</span>
78	        <h4>Website is the atomic unit</h4>
79	        <p>Account holds the plan (capacity). Websites are independent peers, each owning its own domain, files, app, email, DB, SSL, backups.</p>
80	      </div>
81	    </div>
82	  </section>
83	
84	  <!-- 2 -->
85	  <section>
86	    <h2><span class="n">2</span>The data model</h2>
87	    <p class="lead">Clean separation of concerns. The <b>Website</b> and the flexible <b>Mailbox pool</b> are the two ideas everything hangs off.</p>
88	    <div class="ent">
89	      <div class="e"><b>Account</b><span>The customer. Login, wallet, billing.</span></div>
90	      <div class="e"><b>Plan</b><span>Capacity pool: # sites, storage, mailbox pool, DBs, features.</span></div>
91	      <div class="e key"><b>Website ★</b><span>Atomic unit. Its own docroot, app, PHP, SSL, backups, analytics.</span></div>
92	      <div class="e"><b>Domain</b><span>Free subdomain · connected · registered. Points at a website.</span></div>
93	      <div class="e key"><b>Mailbox ★</b><span>you@anydomain. Draws from the plan's shared pool.</span></div>
94	      <div class="e"><b>Database</b><span>Belongs to a website. Auto-made, mostly invisible.</span></div>
95	    </div>
96	    <pre class="dia" style="margin-top:16px">
97	<b>Account</b> ──has one──▶ <b>Plan</b>  <i>(capacity: 10 sites · 100GB · 25 mailboxes · ∞ DBs)</i>
98	   │
99	   ├─owns──▶ <b>Website</b> ──▶ Domain(s) · App · Files · Database(s) · SSL · Backups
100	   │            "my-store"      shop.com          WordPress   auto
101	   │
102	   └─owns──▶ <b>Mailbox pool</b>  <i>(25 total, allocate anywhere)</i>
103	                ├─ sales@shop.com
104	                ├─ hi@blog.com
105	                └─ team@myfolio.com     ← <i>you choose the domain per mailbox</i></pre>
106	  </section>
107	
108	  <!-- 3 -->
109	  <section>
110	    <h2><span class="n">3</span>The logic decisions (locked)</h2>
111	    <div class="grid2">
112	      <div class="card"><h4>Plan = capacity pool</h4><p>Buy a tier, add websites freely up to the cap. Storage, mailboxes, and DBs are shared pools, not per-site allotments.</p></div>
113	      <div class="card"><h4>Flexible email pool ★</h4><p>Plan unlocks N mailboxes. You allocate them across <b>any</b> of your domains — 5 on shop.com, 3 on blog.com — never all pinned to one. Email is a headline feature.</p></div>
114	      <div class="card"><h4>Domains, three ways</h4><p>Instant free <span class="mono">*.sites.nxeon.cloud</span> · connect a domain you own (DNS wizard) · buy one in-app via Namecheap. One bill.</p></div>
115	      <div class="card"><h4>Databases, invisible</h4><p>Auto-created with WordPress, tied to their website. Surfaced only under <b>Advanced</b> for power users.</p></div>
116	    </div>
117	  </section>
118	
119	  <!-- 4 -->
120	  <section>
121	    <h2><span class="n">4</span>The experience</h2>
122	    <p class="lead">One principle kills the clutter: <b>simple by default, power on tap.</b> A grandma-simple happy path; an Advanced layer that never intrudes.</p>
123	    <div class="card">
124	      <h4 style="margin-bottom:12px">Onboarding — live in ~60 seconds</h4>
125	      <div class="flow">
126	        <span class="s">Sign up</span><span class="a">→</span>
127	        <span class="s"><b>Name</b> your site + pick domain <span style="color:var(--faint)">(free subdomain default)</span></span><span class="a">→</span>
128	        <span class="s"><b>What is it?</b> WordPress · Upload · App</span><span class="a">→</span>
129	        <span class="s" style="border-color:rgba(52,211,153,.4)"><b style="color:var(--emerald)">Live</b> on a real URL</span><span class="a">→</span>
130	        <span class="s">Guided checklist: connect domain · add email</span>
131	      </div>
132	    </div>
133	    <div class="card" style="margin-top:16px">
134	      <h4 style="margin-bottom:6px">Per-website console — replaces the cluttered tabs</h4>
135	      <p><b style="color:var(--text)">Up front:</b> Overview (preview, status, Visit, quick actions) · Site · Email · Domain &amp; SSL · Backups.<br>
136	      <b style="color:var(--text)">Behind "Advanced":</b> Files · Database · PHP version · Cron · SSH · Staging.</p>
137	    </div>
138	    <div class="card" style="margin-top:16px">
139	      <h4 style="margin-bottom:6px">Account level (separate from any one site)</h4>
140	      <p>Websites (home) · Domains (registrar/DNS) · Email pool · Billing &amp; plan/usage · Team · Settings.</p>
141	    </div>
142	  </section>
143	
144	  <!-- 5 -->
145	  <section>
146	    <h2><span class="n">5</span>Migrations — the switching lever</h2>
147	    <p class="lead">How people leave competitors. A free, mostly-automated <b>"move my site to Nxeon"</b>: give us your old host login, we pull the files + database, stand it up on a staging URL, you verify, we flip DNS. Headline acquisition feature.</p>
148	  </section>
149	
150	  <!-- 6 -->
151	  <section>
152	    <h2><span class="n">6</span>Plan tiers <span class="pill" style="margin-left:6px">rough — tune together</span></h2>
153	    <table>
154	      <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Websites</th><th>Storage</th><th>Mailbox pool</th><th>For</th></tr>
155	      <tr><td><b>Starter</b></td><td>1</td><td>10 GB</td><td>2</td><td>First site, small biz</td></tr>
156	      <tr><td><b>Business</b> ★</td><td>~10</td><td>100 GB</td><td>25</td><td>The popular one</td></tr>
157	      <tr><td><b>Agency</b></td><td>~25–50</td><td>250 GB</td><td>100</td><td>Freelancers, many clients</td></tr>
158	    </table>
159	  </section>
160	
161	  <!-- 7 -->
162	  <section>
163	    <h2><span class="n">7</span>Build roadmap</h2>
164	    <div class="phase p1">
165	      <h4>Phase 1 — the re-foundation <span class="pill" style="color:var(--emerald);border-color:rgba(52,211,153,.4)">now</span></h4>
166	      <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:14px;margin-top:4px">Data model split (Account/Plan/Website/Domain/Mailbox/DB) · website-first console + Advanced layer · flexible email pool · domains three-ways · clean onboarding. Migrate existing accounts safely.</p>
167	    </div>
168	    <div class="phase">
169	      <h4>Phase 2 — acquisition &amp; agencies</h4>
170	      <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:14px;margin-top:4px">Automated WordPress migration · agency features: manage all client sites, client handoff/transfer, white-label.</p>
171	    </div>
172	    <div class="phase">
173	      <h4>Phase 3 — the smart layer</h4>
174	      <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:14px;margin-top:4px">Claude-assisted setup &amp; troubleshooting ("describe your business → scaffold the site", plain-English fixes).</p>
175	    </div>
176	  </section>
177	
178	  <!-- 8 -->
179	  <section>
180	    <h2><span class="n">8</span>Open questions for you</h2>
181	    <div class="q"><b>1. Existing customers.</b> There's live data (your test accounts + the ad's first signups). Migrate them into the new model automatically (their current domain becomes their first Website), or is a clean break fine while volume is tiny?</div>
182	    <div class="q"><b>2. Plan numbers.</b> Do the tier counts above feel right, or do you have target price points / limits in mind?</div>
183	    <div class="q"><b>3. Storage &amp; mailbox pools.</b> Truly pooled (shared across sites) — confirm — vs any per-site minimums?</div>
184	    <div class="q"><b>4. "Better than everyone" — your gut.</b> What's the ONE thing that, if we nail it, makes people choose us? (Instant-live simplicity? All-in-one bill? Migrations? Email? Something else?)</div>
185	  </section>
186	
187	  <div class="foot">Nxeon Hosting restructure blueprint · draft for review · logic + design + infrastructure</div>
188	</div>
